+# Independent higher-odd spatial review
+
+Disposition: PASS within the stated supplied free Gaussian/AP and uniform quasi-locality premises. This is an analytical review, with no numerical or physical execution.
+
+Reviewed source: `native-uniform-fixed-coupling-stretch/HIGHER_ODD_SPATIAL_SUMMABILITY.md`, SHA256 `545ea28ea75d5bf10b7576fb0a772cd5cafbcbd3ce65fce1532f6032899a32fb`, together with my preceding `REVIEW.md` of the nine-power tail and its parent inverse-moment/domain argument. I did not author this extension.
+
+## Filter and vacuum cancellation
+
+For every integer n, f^(n) is square integrable: smooth cutoff removes the origin and its positive-infinity tail is O(x^(-n-1)). Plancherel supplies every polynomially weighted L2 norm of w. Applying Cauchy–Schwarz with an additional integrable weight (1+t²)^(-1) supplies every stated weighted L1 norm. A possible logarithmic singularity at zero therefore does not invalidate the Bochner integral or the long-time estimate. Scaling w_epsilon(t)=w(epsilon t) gives precisely epsilon^(-p-1).
+
+With the declared negative-time Heisenberg convention, taking the adjoint conjugates w, so its vacuum spectral multiplier is conjugate f_epsilon(-H)=f_epsilon(-H). Positivity of H and f_epsilon(0)=0 make X*Omega zero, including possible zero modes. Consequently omega(X Z_v*) is zero; the anticommutator replacement is valid for non-Hermitian Z. It would not follow for a two-sided inverse filter.
+
+## Infrared and locality estimates
+
+The inverse approximation error is supported below epsilon and bounded by E^-1. Integration by parts of the reviewed tail gives integral E^-2 dmu <= (9C/7) epsilon^7. This uses the inherited absence of a zero-energy atom in Z Omega; the open-interval tail alone would not supply that fact.
+
+For times at most cR, first truncate Z_w to a smaller fraction of R, then propagate into the R/4 ball. Choose c below a fixed Lieb–Robinson velocity margin. Polynomial support-volume prefactors are absorbed by the exponential leakage; rapid initial tails give any fixed inverse power. Constants are independent of epsilon and volume, though not of the requested power. Odd conditional expectations/truncations preserve grading. Disjoint-support anticommutators then vanish exactly. The resulting two operator errors are epsilon^-1 R^-q and epsilon^(-p-1) R^-p, as claimed. No unproved uniform-in-epsilon localization constant is being assumed.
+
+## Summability and limit
+
+For a=7/8 and p=q=32 the exponents are 49/16, 249/8 and 25/8; their minimum exceeds three. Centered representatives of torus sites have the same shortest graph distance from the origin as their centered lattice coordinates, so the bound gives a common summable majorant after zero extension. Fixed-entry convergence from the prior thermodynamic theorem therefore yields strong l1 convergence. Uniform Fourier convergence follows by the l1 bound in the fixed magnetic-cell convention; no Hilbert-space embedding is needed.
+
+The argument establishes this conclusion only for the higher-odd response kernel. It neither proves summability of the full kernel nor identifies the full sixth-order effective Hamiltonian. No correction requested.
